  :adr/body
  "## What was measured

A rename usually removes the old west entry but leaves the old DIRECTORY on
disk. West will never update or recreate those, so they drift quietly and are
invisible to anything that reads `manifest/west.yml`. 39 were identified; the
audit found 41, because one bare name in the list matched two on-disk
directories.

**Nothing was deleted.** This records what deleting would cost, so the decision
can be made on evidence rather than on the word `orphan`.

## Result

- **37 SAFE** -- hold nothing that exists nowhere else
- **4 HOLD WORK**
- **0 UNKNOWN**

Two facts hold across all 41, and both are stronger than expected:

- **Zero unpushed commits anywhere.** Every local branch tip in every orphan --
  including all 33 in `local-murakumo`, 9 in `cloud-murakumo`, 8 in `kawaraban`
  -- was confirmed present server-side in the registered repository. The only
  commits absent from GitHub are three stash commits.
- **Every orphan HEAD is an ancestor of its registered repo's `main`**, 41 of
  41, with `behind_by = 0`. None diverged.

**All 41 pairs share a GitHub repository id**, so every one is a rename
redirect. That had to be checked rather than assumed: a shared root commit is
also what a FORK has, and two pairs elsewhere in this fleet turned out to be
genuinely different repositories -- deleting either would have destroyed a real
project.

## The method matters, because the obvious one gives a false positive

`git log --branches --not --remotes` is the natural way to ask for unpushed
commits, and **it is wrong here**: most of these clones have zero
remote-tracking refs, so that command reports their ENTIRE history as unpushed.
Every branch tip and stash commit was instead tested two ways -- ancestry in the
sibling's local object database, and existence server-side via
`gh api repos/<registered>/commits/<sha>`.

Dirty working trees were checked file by file with `shasum` rather than by
count. All but one are build output (`.cpcache`, `.shadow-cljs`, `__pycache__`)
or bytes identical to the sibling's worktree or the registered repo's `main`.

## The four that hold something

1. **`gftdcojp/local-murakumo`, `stash@{0}`** (`7d827047`, absent from GitHub).
   `worker.cljs` +6/-2: `auth-ok?` extended to accept a second, separately
   rotatable `BABINIKU_PROXY_TOKEN` beside `ANTHROPIC_PROXY_TOKEN`. Not present
   in the registered checkout or on its `main`. The stash message itself says
   drop-forbidden.
2. **`gftdcojp/cloud-murakumo`, `stash@{0}`** (`32dfb542`, absent from GitHub).
   `deps.edn` +3/-1: pins `ed25519` by `:git/url` + `:git/sha` instead of
   `:local/root`. Registered checkout and remote `main` still carry the
   `:local/root` form, so this never landed.
3. **`gftdcojp/shinshi-growth-actor`, `stash@{0}`** (`10c5a441`, absent from
   GitHub). Two untracked blobs -- an earlier `.cljc`/js-fetch variant targeting
   `com.etzhayyim.apps.shinshi-growth.socialPost`. The registered repo has a
   superseding `.clj` port, and the two commits the stash message names as
   superseding it DO exist there. The verdict is conservative on purpose: the
   bytes exist nowhere else, even though the author annotated them as broken.
4. **`gftdcojp/ai-gftd-ongakuka`, untracked `artifacts/`** -- 164 MB, twelve
   rendered audio files the registered sibling does not have. But the sibling
   has the matching `*_input_params.json`, and the registered repo's
   `.gitignore` excludes `artifacts/`, `*.wav`, `*.mp3` -- so this was never
   meant for git and is regenerable output, not lost work.

## Before deleting

For 1-3, land or archive the stashes first, per `manifest/cleanup-workflow.edn`
`:retirement`: export each to `.git/stash-archive-<date>/`, and **re-resolve the
stash index by SHA at drop time**, since a concurrent session can shift
`stash@{0}`. Items 1 and 2 are genuinely unlanded and need a decision on whether
to port them onto the registered checkout; item 3 needs a maintainer to confirm
the author's supersession note.

For 4, decide whether 164 MB of regenerable audio is worth keeping. If it is, it
belongs in B2/DataLad (skill `large-binary-datalad`), not in an orphan checkout.

Two notes on the remaining 37: `gftdcojp/dougaka-actor` is **not a git
repository at all** -- two log files from a crashed outer-loop run, 4 KB. And
`gftdcojp/cloud-manimani` carries one stray unstaged DELETION of
`src/cloud_manimani/xrpc.cljs`, a file that still exists on `main` and in the
registered checkout, so the deletion is the local anomaly rather than the file.

## Why this is worth a document rather than a cleanup

The cheap version of this task is `rm -rf` on 41 directories, and it would have
been almost right -- 37 of 41. The three stashes are the reason it is not: they
are the only bytes in this set that exist nowhere else, they are individually
small, and two of them are unlanded changes to authentication and dependency
pinning. `git fsck --unreachable` can sometimes recover a dropped stash, but
that is luck, not a plan.

Recorded also because the same shape recurs: `manifest/cleanup-workflow.edn`
already requires archiving before dropping, and the reason it exists is that
confidence has previously been used as a substitute for checking."}]
